 {"q": "Why does the rich young man's story matter for reading the Sermon?", "options": ["Because it proves that only the poor can be saved", "Because it shows a sincere law-keeper meeting the Sermon's call to be 'perfect', failing, and Jesus answering 'with God all things are possible'", "Because it is part of the Sermon", "Because it shows Jesus rejecting the commandments"], "answer": 1, "why": "It dramatises the gap between keeping the commandments and the Sermon's 'more', and gives the answer: God's power."},
 {"q": "What does Jesus mean by 'If you want to be perfect' in Matthew 19:21?", "options": ["Flawless in every action", "Whole or complete (teleios, as in 5:48): for this man, freeing his heart from his wealth and following Jesus", "A member of the priesthood", "Someone who has memorised the law"], "answer": 1, "why": "The same word as 5:48; the command ends with 'come, follow me'."},
 {"q": "What is the difference between 'precepts' and 'counsels' in Catholic teaching?", "options": ["Precepts are for priests, counsels for lay people", "Precepts bind everyone (such as love of God and neighbour); counsels (poverty, chastity, obedience) invite some to a fuller way, as in religious orders", "Counsels are older than precepts", "There is no difference"], "answer": 1, "why": "Aquinas, Summa I-II q. 108 a. 4; Vatican II adds that all are called to holiness."},
